window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.6","title":"The Two-Sample t-Test","goal":"Compare exam scores of 30 students who studied with music and 30 who studied in silence, with Welch's and the pooled two-sample t-test.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the two groups","text":"The scores below are built to match the lesson: music averages 74 (SD 8), silence averages 79 (SD 7), 30 students each. Different students are in each group, so the groups are independent.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nmusic = np.array([77.91, 82.44, 77.77, 62.24, 83.23, 78.87, 69.52, 80.15, 78.09, 77.42, 74.9, 79.82, 67.63, 73.08, 70.05, 80.32, 75.01, 71.85, 67.2, 72.18, 74.71, 72.01, 86.93, 84.2, 48.86, 56.67, 72.97, 70.62, 76.66, 76.69])\nsilence = np.array([72.97, 64.46, 82.5, 83.47, 76.26, 80.89, 90.07, 85.63, 83.21, 83.08, 72.47, 84.97, 67.1, 76.11, 75.39, 66.56, 77.62, 89.61, 76.05, 70.12, 77.75, 84.22, 79.7, 78.36, 68.74, 81.8, 81.7, 87.55, 88.56, 83.08])\n\nfor name, g in [('music', music), ('silence', silence)]:\n    print(f'{name}: n = {len(g)}, mean = {g.mean():.2f}, sd = {g.std(ddof=1):.2f}')","out":"music: n = 30, mean = 74.00, sd = 8.00\nsilence: n = 30, mean = 79.00, sd = 7.00","figs":[]},{"title":"Welch's t-test (the safe default)","text":"equal_var=False gives Welch's test, which does not assume the two groups have the same spread. The degrees of freedom are not a whole number because they are adjusted for the unequal spreads.","code":"welch = stats.ttest_ind(music, silence, equal_var=False)\n\nprint(f't  = {welch.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'df = {welch.df:.1f}')\nprint(f'p  = {welch.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"t  = -2.576\ndf = 57.0\np  = 0.0126","figs":[]},{"title":"The pooled t-test","text":"equal_var=True blends the two variances into one. Here the group sizes are equal and the spreads are similar, so the two tests agree closely. They would not agree if the smaller group were also the noisier one.","code":"pooled = stats.ttest_ind(music, silence, equal_var=True)\n\nprint(f't  = {pooled.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'df = {pooled.df:.0f}')\nprint(f'p  = {pooled.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"t  = -2.576\ndf = 58\np  = 0.0126","figs":[]},{"title":"Confidence interval for the difference","text":"The test says whether the gap is real, and the interval says how big it might be. It is for music minus silence, so a fully negative interval means music scores are lower.","code":"ci = welch.confidence_interval(confidence_level=0.95)\ndiff = music.mean() - silence.mean()\n\nprint(f'difference (music - silence) = {diff:.2f}')\nprint(f'95% CI: ({ci.low:.2f}, {ci.high:.2f})')\nprint('interval contains 0?', ci.low <= 0 <= ci.high)","out":"difference (music - silence) = -5.00\n95% CI: (-8.89, -1.11)\ninterval contains 0? False","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"music <- c(77.91, 82.44, 77.77, 62.24, 83.23, 78.87, 69.52, 80.15, 78.09, 77.42, 74.9, 79.82, 67.63, 73.08, 70.05, 80.32, 75.01, 71.85, 67.2, 72.18, 74.71, 72.01, 86.93, 84.2, 48.86, 56.67, 72.97, 70.62, 76.66, 76.69)\nsilence <- c(72.97, 64.46, 82.5, 83.47, 76.26, 80.89, 90.07, 85.63, 83.21, 83.08, 72.47, 84.97, 67.1, 76.11, 75.39, 66.56, 77.62, 89.61, 76.05, 70.12, 77.75, 84.22, 79.7, 78.36, 68.74, 81.8, 81.7, 87.55, 88.56, 83.08)\n\nt.test(music, silence)                    # Welch (the R default)\nt.test(music, silence, var.equal = TRUE)  # pooled","expect":"Welch: t = -2.5762, df = 56.997, p-value = 0.01261, 95% CI -8.886 to -1.114; pooled: t = -2.5762, df = 58, p-value = 0.01256, 95% CI -8.885 to -1.115","note":"R's t.test uses Welch's test by default, so you must add var.equal = TRUE to get the pooled version. Both outputs also print the 95% confidence interval for the difference."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Compare Means > Independent-Samples T Test","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / group score.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 77.91 1 82.44 1 77.77 1 62.24 1 83.23 1 78.87 1 69.52 1 80.15 1 78.09 1 77.42\n1 74.9 1 79.82 1 67.63 1 73.08 1 70.05 1 80.32 1 75.01 1 71.85 1 67.2 1 72.18\n1 74.71 1 72.01 1 86.93 1 84.2 1 48.86 1 56.67 1 72.97 1 70.62 1 76.66 1 76.69\n2 72.97 2 64.46 2 82.5 2 83.47 2 76.26 2 80.89 2 90.07 2 85.63 2 83.21 2 83.08\n2 72.47 2 84.97 2 67.1 2 76.11 2 75.39 2 66.56 2 77.62 2 89.61 2 76.05 2 70.12\n2 77.75 2 84.22 2 79.7 2 78.36 2 68.74 2 81.8 2 81.7 2 87.55 2 88.56 2 83.08\nEND DATA.\nT-TEST GROUPS=group(1 2)\n  /VARIABLES=score\n  /CRITERIA=CI(.95).","note":"SPSS prints both tests in one table: the 'Equal variances assumed' row is the pooled test and 'Equal variances not assumed' is Welch. Group 1 = music, group 2 = silence."},"stata":{"code":"ttesti 30 74 8 30 79 7              // pooled (Stata's default)\nttesti 30 74 8 30 79 7, unequal     // Welch-type test, same df as R and Python","note":"ttesti runs the test from group size, mean and SD, so no data entry is needed (with raw data use: ttest score, by(group) and add unequal). Stata is pooled unless you add unequal. The lesson's rounded 74, 8, 79, 7 give t = -2.576, and Python's built data differ only in the 4th digit."},"try":["Change the silence mean to 77 (edit the data by subtracting 2 from every silence score) and predict what happens to p.","Cut each group to its first 10 scores (music[:10], silence[:10]) and predict whether the result stays significant."]});
